# Rank 1338 / CVE-2017-5929: logback's socket-based log receivers read
# serialized ILoggingEvent objects off a network socket with a plain
# java.io.ObjectInputStream, so anything that can connect to the listening
# port controls which classes get instantiated -- the textbook Java native
# deserialization gadget-chain shape (CWE-502). SocketNode.run() opens
# `new ObjectInputStream(new BufferedInputStream(socket.getInputStream()))`
# and loops on `ois.readObject()`; SocketReceiver and
# RemoteAppenderStreamClient carry the identical pattern, and logback-access
# has its own SocketNode copy.
#
# The fixing commit ("harden serialization") adds
# logback-core/src/main/java/ch/qos/logback/core/net/HardenedObjectInputStream.java,
# an ObjectInputStream subclass whose resolveClass() override throws
# InvalidClassException for any class name outside an explicit allowlist plus
# the java.lang/java.util packages. That commit only introduces the class and
# uses it from a test; the receivers are switched over one commit later in
# 979b042c, which replaces SocketNode's ObjectInputStream field with
# HardenedLoggingEventInputStream (a HardenedObjectInputStream subclass
# carrying logback's own event-class allowlist) and does the same for
# RemoteAppenderStreamClient. Both revisions below are taken from those two
# points.
#
# ATTENTION, and the reason is that the change is hardening rather than the
# removal of a sink, so nothing shipped discriminates the two revisions.
# VYQL-DESER-001 fires on the vulnerable revision at exactly the CVE's sites
# -- SocketNode.java:83, SocketReceiver.java:162,
# RemoteAppenderStreamClient.java:95 -- and it fires on the patched revision
# at the same three sites (SocketNode shifts to :82), five findings on each
# side with an identical sink set. `vyql match` shows why:
# deserialization129 in vyql/bindings/java/m420f6b67.vyql keys on
# `callee.method == "readObject"` alone, so
# `hardenedLoggingEventInputStream.readObject()` is labelled
# code.Deserialization exactly as `ois.readObject()` is, and
# `socket.getInputStream()` is a code.HttpInput source either way
# (httpInput29, same file). `vyql explain` reports both of the rule's
# suppressing clauses unsatisfied on both revisions -- "no neutralizing
# control dominates the path; none found anywhere on flows" -- because the
# only Java core.SafeDeserialization binding is SnakeYAML's Yaml.safeLoad
# (safeDeserialization23 in vyql/bindings/packages/java/m9ef49b0f.vyql) and
# nothing covers ObjectInputStream at all.
#
# This is the control gap recorded at rank 1089, whose own fix ships
# the readObject()/readUnshared() sink knowing no allowlist control existed to
# balance it. This rank adds a second requirement to that entry rather than a
# new one. Two things would discriminate these revisions, and neither is
# mechanical:
#
#   1. A control recognizing that a resolveClass()/readClassDescriptor()
#      override rejects class names outside a bounded set. "Overrides
#      resolveClass" on its own is not a safety signal and must not be used as
#      one: rank 1089's *vulnerable* revision overrode readClassDescriptor to
#      resolve whatever the stream named, so a control keyed on the override
#      alone would suppress that rank's true positive -- the same self-
#      endorsing-control failure rank 1051 settled for core.AllowlistValidation.
#      The judgement has to come from the override's body comparing the
#      incoming name against a bounded set and throwing
#      (InvalidClassException here, NotSerializableException at 1089), which is
#      presence analysis over an arbitrary method body.
#   2. Attribution of that class-level control to readObject() calls whose
#      receiver is statically an instance of the hardened subclass, across
#      files. Neither `unless path coveredBy` nor `unless endpoint coveredBy`
#      can reach it: HardenedObjectInputStream.resolveClass sits in
#      logback-core, off the socket-to-readObject path in logback-classic, and
#      is never on any flow the rule sees. That is a new control-attribution
#      relation, not a binding.
#
# Narrowing the sink instead is not available either. The generic, documented
# Java allowlist hooks -- ObjectInputStream.setObjectInputFilter/
# ObjectInputFilter.Config (JEP 290) and commons-io's
# ValidatingObjectInputStream.accept -- are both worth binding as
# core.SafeDeserialization on their own merit, but neither appears here:
# logback's fix predates JDK 9 and rolls its own subclass. A binding naming
# HardenedObjectInputStream or HardenedLoggingEventInputStream would name a
# class that exists only in logback, failing the genericity test.
#
# Both revisions therefore assert the finding: the vulnerable one as a true
# positive, the patched one as the false positive the missing control would
# suppress. No definition change attempted.
#
# Diagnosing the patched revision surfaced a second, separate engine gap, the
# reason the patched revision's report depends on the shape of the hardened
# class rather than on anything about the allowlist: taint entering a resolved
# user-defined constructor is dropped when the constructor's only use of the
# argument is an explicit constructor invocation, `super(in)`. The pair below
# isolates it -- identical source, identical `readObject` sink, differing only
# in whether the socket stream reaches ObjectInputStream directly or through a
# one-line subclass constructor that delegates with `super(in)`. The subclass
# form reports nothing, and `vyql trace` shows the source with no outgoing
# edge at all ("0 reach a sink, 1 dead-end", no frontier), because
# `explicit_constructor_invocation` has no case in
# internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/java.go, so `super(in)` never becomes a
# flow and the constructed instance never inherits its argument's taint.
# logback's own patched revision escapes this only because
# HardenedObjectInputStream and HardenedLoggingEventInputStream each carry two
# overloaded constructors: the call does not resolve to a single body, so the
# opaque arg-to-result propagation applies instead and the sink is reached.
# Not mechanical: making `super(...)`/`this(...)` propagate its arguments to
# the enclosing instance is a new dataflow relation on every Java class that
# delegates to a base constructor, not a corrected extraction path, and it
# widens taint rather than narrowing it, so it needs the OWASP Java corpus to
# judge. It also cannot fix this rank -- it would make the patched revision
# report on more shapes, not fewer.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions fire identically

test "rank1338: SocketNode reads logging events off a socket through a plain ObjectInputStream"
  lang java
  file ch/qos/logback/classic/net/SocketNode.java
  expect VYQL-DESER-001
  code
  ```
  package ch.qos.logback.classic.net;

  import java.io.BufferedInputStream;
  import java.io.IOException;
  import java.io.ObjectInputStream;
  import java.net.Socket;

  import ch.qos.logback.classic.Logger;
  import ch.qos.logback.classic.LoggerContext;
  import ch.qos.logback.classic.spi.ILoggingEvent;

  public class SocketNode implements Runnable {

      Socket socket;
      LoggerContext context;
      ObjectInputStream ois;

      Logger logger;
      boolean closed = false;

      public SocketNode(Socket socket, LoggerContext context) {
          this.socket = socket;
          this.context = context;
          logger = context.getLogger(SocketNode.class);
      }

      public void run() {
          try {
              ois = new ObjectInputStream(new BufferedInputStream(socket.getInputStream()));
          } catch (Exception e) {
              logger.error("Could not open ObjectInputStream to " + socket, e);
              closed = true;
          }

          ILoggingEvent event;
          Logger remoteLogger;

          try {
              while (!closed) {
                  event = (ILoggingEvent) ois.readObject();
                  remoteLogger = context.getLogger(event.getLoggerName());
                  if (remoteLogger.isEnabledFor(event.getLevel())) {
                      remoteLogger.callAppenders(event);
                  }
              }
          } catch (IOException e) {
              logger.info("Caught java.io.IOException: " + e);
          } catch (Exception e) {
              logger.error("Unexpected exception. Closing connection.", e);
          }
          close();
      }

      void close() {
          closed = true;
      }
  }
  ```

